schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Type vs. Tag (metadata discipline)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A type says what something *is* (a document has exactly one type,
    e.g. guide, meeting note, decision, person); a tag says how something
    might be classified or filtered (a document can have many tags, e.g.
    governance, solar, draft). Confusing the two creates ontology sprawl.
  source_traditions:
    - >-
      the Regen Web3 Toolkit's own internal ontology principle 11 ("type /
      tag discipline")
    - folksonomy research on collaborative/uncontrolled tagging
    - library/information-science controlled-vocabulary practice
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Presented as "the one distinction to get right" before designing any
    metadata schema — the article states plainly that this toolkit's own
    architecture "makes this its eleventh cross-cutting principle" and
    warns that confusing tags with types creates ontology sprawl.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - controlled vocabulary (the fix once type/tag is separated — a fixed set of approved tag values)
    - folksonomy (uncontrolled, emergent tagging — the failure mode this distinction guards against)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      type ≠ tag — one type per document (routes it) vs. many tags per
      document (classifies/filters it); this is the article's central
      claim
  tensions:
    - >-
      light vocabulary control (recommended) vs. unmoderated folksonomy
      tagging — research cited ("Folksonomy," Wikipedia) shows unmoderated
      tags drift into inconsistency, but heavy-handed control kills
      contribution; the article does not fully resolve where the line sits,
      only that some control beats none
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Presenting type/tag discipline as a simple binary rule undersells how
    hard the "what is the type" question can get for genuinely hybrid
    documents (e.g. a meeting note that is also a decision record) — the
    article's own examples are the clean cases, not the ambiguous ones a
    real commons will hit.
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/metadata-that-matters.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
